entary Results, Supplementary Methods, Supplementary Figs. 1–4 and Supplementary References.\n\n### Reporting Summary (download PDF )\n\n### Supplementary Tables 1–18 (download ZIP )\n\nSee Supplementary Information file for Supplementary Table legends.\n\n## About this article\n\n### Cite this article\n\nSun, R., Qian, L., Li, Y. et al. An operational perturbation proteomics-based virtual cell model. Nature (2026). https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-11001-9\n\n  * Received: 17 August 2024\n\n  * Accepted: 07 August 2026\n\n  * Published: 09 September 2026\n\n  * Version of record: 09 September 2026\n\n  * DOI (Digital Object Identifier): https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-11001-9--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn operational perturbation proteomics-based virtual cell model - GoKawiil (https://gokawiil.com/article/341197)\nciteturn793search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: yesterday; # An operational perturbation proteomics-based virtual cell model\n\nGoKawiil Tech News\n\n← Back to articles\n\n# An operational perturbation proteomics-based virtual cell model\n\n2026-09-09 | original\n\nWhy This Matters\n\nThe push to build an AI \"virtual cell\" has so far been dominated by single-cell transcriptomics foundation models; this work argues for grounding virtual cell models in perturbation proteomics, the layer where drug effects and cellular function actually play out. Because proteins are the targets of most therapeutics, a proteomics-anchored, perturbation-driven model could make in-silico cell simulation more actionable for drug discovery. It also underscores that the bottleneck is data generation in closed-loop with modeling, not just bigger models.\n\nKey Takeaways\n\n  * Virtual cell efforts are shifting from RNA-centric foundation models toward proteomics and perturbation response data.\n  * Predicting how cells react to perturbations, across contexts, is emerging as the key benchmark for these 